WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:03.853
Sales wants Cedar Vale's solar-support proposal reviewed.

00:03.953 --> 00:06.697
But there's also a battery-monitoring agreement.

00:06.797 --> 00:11.013
Which record is right, what does the price cover, and who acts next?

00:11.113 --> 00:13.047
Let's make that handoff clear.

00:14.133 --> 00:19.309
Here's how to use HubSpot Contracts to check a draft and hand it over for review.

00:19.409 --> 00:21.193
The deal tracks the sale.

00:21.293 --> 00:26.960
The Contract brings together its commercial details, including changes and renewals.

00:27.060 --> 00:29.164
We'll finish with a checked draft;

00:29.264 --> 00:31.432
customer acceptance comes later.

00:32.533 --> 00:36.237
BeaconGrid provides solar support to Cedar Vale Foods.

00:36.337 --> 00:39.124
Alex Morgan is Cedar Vale's contact.

00:39.224 --> 00:46.469
Our source is Solar Support Proposal, version one: one thousand US dollars a month for twelve months.

00:46.569 --> 00:48.908
Keep that proposal beside the draft.

00:49.008 --> 00:51.965
It's our reference for every check that follows.

00:53.067 --> 00:56.067
In HubSpot, open Revenue, then Contracts.

00:56.167 --> 01:02.559
This demo uses the direct-edit beta, so you'll need that access and permission to edit Contracts.

01:02.659 --> 01:06.725
Open Cedar Vale, Solar Support, and check the Draft label.

01:06.825 --> 01:09.655
Battery Monitoring is a separate agreement.

01:09.755 --> 01:11.901
Same customer, different service.

01:12.001 --> 01:14.020
Then choose Edit contract.

01:15.100 --> 01:17.353
First, match the people and the source.

01:17.453 --> 01:22.544
BeaconGrid is the seller, Cedar Vale the customer, and Alex the contact.

01:22.644 --> 01:25.537
Check the associated Solar Support deal too.

01:25.637 --> 01:28.125
The link helps us find the relationship;

01:28.225 --> 01:30.927
it doesn't prove every field stays in sync.

01:31.027 --> 01:36.843
If the proposal and draft disagree, pause and ask Sales to confirm the correct terms.

01:37.933 --> 01:40.464
Now the number that can cause confusion.

01:40.564 --> 01:46.657
This line is one Solar Support service at one thousand dollars a month, for twelve payments.

01:46.757 --> 01:50.419
That makes twelve thousand dollars across the proposed term.

01:50.519 --> 01:53.561
It's the term value, not a bill due today.

01:53.661 --> 01:57.813
Check the quantity, frequency and payment count before moving on.

01:58.900 --> 02:07.361
Next, check when the service runs: the first of November, twenty twenty-six, through the thirty-first of October, twenty twenty-seven.

02:07.461 --> 02:11.315
Compare the effective date and preview with the proposal too.

02:11.415 --> 02:13.711
These dates match in our example.

02:13.811 --> 02:18.880
In another agreement, check their meaning rather than assuming they must be identical.

02:19.967 --> 02:25.719
Finance has a separate billing process in this example, so HubSpot Billing stays off.

02:25.819 --> 02:33.085
Read the message on screen: this Contract won't create invoices or collect payments with billing disabled.

02:33.185 --> 02:35.161
We've checked the commercial record.

02:35.261 --> 02:39.371
We haven't connected the billing system or authorized an invoice.

02:40.467 --> 02:44.533
Before handing it over, return to the record and reopen the draft.

02:44.633 --> 02:48.999
Are the customer, service, price, payment count and dates still right?

02:49.099 --> 02:50.605
Is Billing still off?

02:50.705 --> 02:54.559
If you changed a value, confirm it was saved before leaving.

02:54.659 --> 02:58.491
If a value is missing or different, stop and resolve it.

02:58.591 --> 03:00.972
The result we want is a checked draft.

03:02.067 --> 03:11.083
Now the Sales manager has something specific to review: Solar Support Proposal, version one, the matching draft, and the checked terms.

03:11.183 --> 03:15.676
Sales Ops owns the next action: request that internal review.

03:15.776 --> 03:18.072
We haven't submitted it in this demo.

03:18.172 --> 03:24.244
If Sales changes the proposal, update the source version and repeat the affected checks.

03:24.344 --> 03:28.453
Customer acceptance and billing approval remain separate steps.

03:29.533 --> 03:32.512
Where do PandaDoc and Docusign fit?

03:32.612 --> 03:36.508
You can keep using them for document preparation and signing.

03:36.608 --> 03:38.435
The deal follows the sale;

03:38.535 --> 03:41.343
the signed document records the agreement;

03:41.443 --> 03:45.019
the HubSpot Contract holds its commercial details.

03:45.119 --> 03:52.449
For an external agreement, those details need a create or import step, or a separately verified integration.

03:52.549 --> 03:58.536
An accepted current HubSpot quote has its own automatic Contract route when configured.

03:59.633 --> 04:01.503
Here's where workflows help.

04:01.603 --> 04:08.379
Document activity, such as completion, can trigger an internal notification or a review task.

04:08.479 --> 04:14.593
Once the right terms are in the Contract, its renewal date can drive reminders and follow-up.

04:14.693 --> 04:19.293
Think of two connected stages: the signing system starts the handoff;

04:19.393 --> 04:22.479
the Contract supports the ongoing relationship.

04:22.579 --> 04:27.520
Automating the Contract-writing step needs its own configured integration.

04:27.620 --> 04:30.428
We'll keep those setup steps in the PDF.

04:31.533 --> 04:38.672
Cedar Vale's Solar Support draft now has a clear source, understandable terms and a next owner for review.

04:38.772 --> 04:40.791
That's the handoff to aim for.

04:40.891 --> 04:46.813
Want the workflow steps for connecting PandaDoc or Docusign to HubSpot Contracts?

04:46.913 --> 04:49.551
Comment WORKFLOW below for the PDF.
